Static Keyword in Java

-------------------------------

What is static:
- static means the member belongs to the CLASS itself, not to any object
- memory is allocated only once when the class is loaded, shared across all instances
- can be accessed without creating an object: ClassName.member

-------------------------------

Static Variable (class variable):
- shared among ALL instances of the class
- if one object changes it, the change is visible to all other objects
- opposite of instance variable, which each object owns separately

  example:
      class Student {
          String name;         // instance variable — each object has its own
          static String school = "MIT";  // static — shared by all Student objects
      }
      // if school is changed, it changes for every Student

-------------------------------

Static Method:
- belongs to the class, called without creating an object
- can only access static variables and other static methods directly
- cannot access instance variables or instance methods (no object context)
- cannot be overridden (resolved at compile time, not runtime)

  example:
      class MathUtils {
          static int square(int n) { return n * n; }
      }
      MathUtils.square(5); // called without creating an object

  real example: Math.sqrt(), Math.abs() — no object needed, just utility logic

-------------------------------

Static Block:
- runs once when the class is first loaded into memory, before main()
- used to initialize static variables that need more than a simple assignment

  example:
      class Config {
          static String mode;
          static {
              mode = "production"; // or load from file, parse, validate, etc.
          }
      }

-------------------------------

Static vs Instance — key difference in objects:

  class Counter {
      static int total = 0;  // shared — counts across ALL objects
      int id;                // instance — unique per object

      Counter() {
          total++;           // every new object increments the shared counter
          id = total;        // each object gets its own id
      }
  }

  Counter a = new Counter(); // total=1, a.id=1
  Counter b = new Counter(); // total=2, b.id=2
  Counter c = new Counter(); // total=3, c.id=3
  // all three objects share total, each has its own id

-------------------------------

Rules to remember:
- static method → can access: static variables, static methods
                → cannot access: instance variables, instance methods
- instance method → can access everything (static and instance)
- static members cannot be overridden, only hidden